Command: without a timeout can hang a cron job
indefinitely. A deployment script using `curl` to poll a service
can hang CI. A monitoring probe using `curl` without a timeout can
hide actual outages — because the probe itself is stuck waiting.

## The fix

Always pass `--max-time`:

```bash
curl --max-time 10 https://example.com/api
```

`--max-time` is the *total* time cap, including connection,
handshake, request, and response. It's the most important curl
flag you've never used.

Optionally also `--connect-timeout` for finer control:

```bash
curl --connect-timeout 5 --max-time 30 https://example.com/api
```

- `--connect-timeout 5` — give up if the TCP connect takes > 5s
- `--max-time 30` — give up if the whole transaction takes > 30s

## Make this permanent

Alias curl with safe defaults in your shell config:

```bash
alias curl='curl --max-time 30 --connect-timeout 10'
```

The original is still available as `\curl` or `command curl` if you
genuinely need no timeout.

